Elaborating his submissions he submits that Section 7 (3) reads as follows:-
"7. Prohibitions of manufacture, sale, etc., of certain articles of food.--No person shall himself or by any person on his behalf manufacture for sale, or store, sell or distribute-- (i) any adulterated food;
(ii) any misbranded food;
(iii) any article of food for the sale of which a licence is prescribed, except in accordance with the conditions of the licence;
Referring to the word prescribed used in sub Section 3 of Section 7, he submits that word prescribed used in aforesaid Section shall be meant to be prescribed by rules, therefore, in order to record the guilt of the revisionist under section 7 (3) of Food Adulteration Act, 1954, it was incumbent upon the Trial Court to record a finding as to under which Rules, requirement of licence was mandatory and was not possessed by the revisionist.
